\description{
\code{p_splines()} smooths the log death rates over age and year with a
Poisson P-spline surface and forecasts them by extending the surface over
the forecast years (Currie, Durban and Eilers 2004). The code follows the
reference implementation, \code{Mort2Dsmooth()} and \code{predict.Mort2Dsmooth()}
of the R package MortalitySmooth 2.3.4 (Camarda), and reproduces its
fitted values, forecasts and standard errors for given smoothing
parameters.
}
\details{
\strong{Model.} The deaths of age \code{x} and year \code{t} are Poisson with mean
\verb{E[x, t] m[x, t]}. The log rates are a tensor-product B-spline surface,
\verb{log m = Ba A t(By)}, with B-splines of degree \code{deg} on equally spaced
knots over age (\code{Ba}) and over years (\code{By}) and a matrix of coefficients
\code{A}. Differences of order \code{pord} between neighbouring coefficients are
penalised, over age with weight \code{lambda[1]} and over years with weight
\code{lambda[2]}. The coefficients maximise the penalised Poisson
log-likelihood (penalised scoring algorithm, computed in array form).
The smoothing parameters minimise the BIC (default) or the AIC, with the
effective dimension (the trace of the hat matrix) as the number of
parameters.

\strong{Forecast.} The forecast is part of the fit. The basis over years is
extended by B-splines on the same knot spacing beyond the last forecast
year (and below the first year, as \code{predict.Mort2Dsmooth()} places the
knots), and the forecast years enter with weight 0: they carry no data,
and their coefficients follow from the penalty. With \code{pord = 2} the
coefficients continue about linearly, so each age continues the trend of
its last years; the order of the penalty determines the form of the
forecast. The model is fitted again on the extended basis with the
smoothing parameters of the fit, as \code{predict.Mort2Dsmooth()} does. The
penalty over age also acts on the added coefficients, so the fitted rates
of the data years change slightly, and the forecast depends a little on
the horizon through the last knot (up to 0.02 on the log scale for
England and Wales men, ages 30-100, 10 years ahead).

\strong{Uncertainty.} Draws come from the normal approximation of the
coefficients, with the covariance \verb{(B'WB + P)^-1} of the penalised
likelihood (\code{W} the fitted deaths, \code{P} the penalty). MortalitySmooth
computes the standard errors of forecast log rates from this covariance.
Over the forecast years the penalty acts as a prior: the variance grows
with the horizon, the faster the smaller \code{lambda[2]}. Draws are paths of
the whole surface, correlated over ages and years as in the model. They
describe the expected rates; predictive draws
(\code{mt_forecast(predictive = TRUE)}) add Poisson variation of the deaths.
With \code{overdispersion = TRUE}, the dispersion \code{psi2} (deviance divided by
the number of cells minus the effective dimension) enters the choice of
the smoothing parameters and the penalty, as in
\code{Mort2Dsmooth(overdispersion = TRUE)}, and the covariance is
\verb{psi2 (B'WB + psi2 P)^-1}. MortalitySmooth's \code{predict()} instead refits
the forecast without \code{psi2} in the penalty and multiplies the standard
errors by \code{sqrt(psi2)}. For England and Wales men (ages 30-100,
1961-2011), the two forecasts differ by up to 0.08 on the log scale ten
years ahead.

\strong{Dispersion by age.} \code{overdispersion = "age"} replaces the Poisson
assumption by the first two moments of the deaths, \verb{E[D] = E m} and
\verb{var(D) = phi[x] E[D]}, with a dispersion \code{phi[x]} for each age that
varies smoothly over age (Djeundje and Currie 2011, eq. 2.1; Model 3,
eq. 5.26). Rates and dispersions are estimated by the bias corrected
scheme of the paper (section 4):
\enumerate{
\item Start with \code{phi = 1} at every age.
\item Fit the rates by penalised quasi-likelihood: the scoring algorithm
with the weights of the cells divided by their dispersion (eq. 3.13).
The smoothing parameters minimise the criterion of the scaled
deviance: the deviance of each cell divided by its dispersion, plus
\code{log(n)} (BIC, eq. 3.16) or 2 (AIC) times the effective dimension.
\item For each age, compute the bias-corrected mean Pearson statistic
\code{d[x]}: the sum of \code{(D - mu)^2 / mu} over its years, divided by its
number of cells \code{n[x]} minus its contribution to the effective
dimension, the sum of its diagonal elements of the hat matrix (eq.
4.20 and 4.21).
\item Smooth the statistics over age by penalised quasi-likelihood with
\code{E[d] = phi}, \verb{var(d) = tau phi^2 / n[x]} and the link \verb{-1 / phi = Ba beta} (eq. 4.22): the B-splines over age of the rates and a
difference penalty of order \code{pord[1]} (eq. 5.28). The smoothing
parameter minimises the scaled BIC of this fit.
\item Repeat steps 2 to 4 until no dispersion changes by more than 1e-5
relative to its value (section 5.2.2), at most 50 times.
}

The forecast fit keeps the dispersions of the ages in the weights, and
the draws come from the covariance \verb{(B'WB + P)^-1} with \code{W} the fitted
deaths divided by their dispersion. In the paper's example (CMI assured
lives, ages 40-90, section 5.2.3) the dispersion down-weights the fit to
the data and the selected surface is smoother: the effective dimension
is 46 under the Poisson assumption, 36 with one dispersion and 33 with
the dispersion by age, and the confidence intervals of the forecast are
narrower. With a single age, \code{"age"} gives one dispersion, the
bias-corrected mean Pearson statistic (eq. 4.20). The fit selects the
smoothing parameters in every round, so it takes several times as long
as the Poisson fit.

\strong{Data.} Ages are placed at the midpoints of their cells, an open
interval at its lower bound plus half the width of the interval below it
(as in \code{\link[=mt_smooth]{mt_smooth()}}). Cells with missing deaths or exposures, and cells
without exposure, get weight 0, so the surface interpolates them.
\code{infant = TRUE} gives the first year of life a row of coefficients of
its own, penalised over years but not connected over age with the
following ages (Camarda 2019, section 3.1); it applies when the grid
starts with age group 0-1 and has at least four age groups.

\strong{Package-specific choices (reference unknown).} By default the knots
are about five years of age and five calendar years apart, \code{floor(n / 5)} intervals for \code{n} ages or years as in MortalitySmooth; for age groups
wider than one year, \code{n} is the number of single years that the cell
ages span. The smoothing parameters are searched on a grid with steps of
1 on the log10 scale, followed by a continuous search near the best grid
point (MortalitySmooth: two grid searches with steps of 2 and 0.5).
With \code{overdispersion = "age"}: \code{tau} is the mean Pearson statistic of the
smooth of step 4, \code{sum(n[x] (d[x] - phi[x])^2 / phi[x]^2)} divided by the
number of ages minus its effective dimension, alternated with the choice
of its smoothing parameter as \code{psi2} with \code{overdispersion = TRUE} (at
most 5 times; the paper estimates \code{tau} without stating how); that
smoothing parameter is searched over \code{lambda_range} as those of the
rates; ages without data get weight 0 in step 4, so the smooth gives
their dispersion; predictive draws add Poisson variation of the deaths,
as with \code{overdispersion = TRUE}, because the quasi-likelihood defines
the mean and variance of the deaths but no distribution, and the paper
gives no predictive distribution. The paper's full extended
quasi-likelihood scheme (mean deviances by age, eq. 2.6) is not offered:
it underestimates the dispersion (section 4, Figure 4), and the paper
reports its results with the bias corrected scheme.
